Trauma during work duties, Mechanism linked to work tasks, Temporary/permanent work disability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-014
Reason for Visit: Acute traumatic crush injury and laceration sustained while operating heavy industrial machinery.
Location: Industrial manufacturing plant (First Aid Room)
Present at Visit: Patient, Occupational Health & Safety (OHS) officer
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a worker who sustained a severe crush injury and deep laceration to the dominant hand in a hydraulic press. Urgent assessment was required for acute hemorrhage control, evaluation of neurovascular compromise, and immediate pain management prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My hand got caught in the press, it's bleeding everywhere, and I can't feel my fingers."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an acute traumatic injury occurring strictly during the performance of work-related duties approximately 30 minutes ago. The mechanism involves a crush injury associated with heavy machinery operation (hydraulic press malfunction). The patient reports sudden, agonizing pain, followed by profuse bleeding and a loss of sensation in the index and middle fingers. The symptoms explicitly match the known hazards described in the specific workplace safety protocols. The patient is highly distressed about the temporary or permanent disability directly impairing the ability to return to their manual occupation, exhibiting an acute stress reaction.
Functional Status: Acutely incapacitated by severe hand trauma
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat to limb/digit viability, massive psychological distress regarding loss of livelihood, potential workplace safety breaches

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic, gripping injured hand tightly, visibly shaking
Physical Exam: Right dominant hand reveals a deep, 6cm ragged laceration across the dorsal aspect of the index and middle metacarpophalangeal joints, with exposed underlying tendons and visible bone fragments. Profuse venous bleeding is present. Capillary refill is delayed (>4 seconds) in the affected digits. Sensation to light touch is absent distal to the laceration. Diagnostic tetrad is met: trauma during work duties, mechanism linked to work tasks, and an immediate temporary/permanent work disability.
Environmental Observations: Industrial setting, noisy machinery, patient sitting in a brightly lit first aid room with blood-soaked towels
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. No allergies. Last tetanus booster > 10 years ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Occupational injuries (Complex crush laceration with neurovascular compromise) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Simple Superficial Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exposed bone, tendon involvement, and objective loss of distal sensation and perfusion.
2. Isolated Phalanx Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The massive soft tissue disruption and profound bleeding indicating a combined osteo-integumentary-neurovascular injury.
3. Factitious Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The witnessed nature of the event in an industrial setting, the severity of the crush, and immediate OHS reporting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient suffered a severe, acute, high-energy industrial crush injury to the dominant hand resulting in complex lacerations, exposed deep structures, and objective signs of distal ischemia and nerve damage. Delaying medical assessment would absolutely result in irreversible tissue necrosis, exsanguination, and permanent loss of digit function. The urgent after-hours intervention was critical to provide immediate hemorrhage control with pressure dressings, administer potent parenteral analgesia, and rapidly expedite transfer to a tertiary facility with specialized hand surgery capabilities.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate application of direct pressure and elevation to control hemorrhage. Administration of intramuscular opiate analgesia (e.g., Morphine) and anti-emetic. Administration of adult dTpa (tetanus) vaccine. Strict NBM status.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping your hand elevated continuously above your heart, you instantly reduce the blood pressure in the wound, which stops the bleeding and significantly decreases the throbbing pain.
Home-Service (Optional): Formal documentation for worker's compensation and OHS incident reporting initiated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bulky, sterile pressure dressing applied.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department and on-call orthopedic/plastic surgery team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority ambulance transfer to a trauma center capable of microsurgery. Do not attempt to clean the wound deeply or remove embedded debris on-site to avoid further vascular damage.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The hand requires emergency surgical exploration and repair in the operating theatre. Recovery will be prolonged and require extensive physiotherapy.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your hand raised high. Do not eat or drink anything, as you will need general anesthesia for the surgery tonight.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The machine has damaged the skin, tendons, and possibly the blood vessels in your hand. The strong painkiller will make the ambulance ride tolerable, but only surgery can fix the structures.
Actionable Steps: Do not look at the wound under the dressing. Take slow, deep breaths to manage the adrenaline. The hospital surgeons are experts in fixing these exact injuries.

